Website and CV workflow
=======================

Sources
-------

The top-level .qmd files are the website pages. _quarto.yml controls navigation,
HTML styling, and the ignored docs/ output directory. Pushing main triggers
.github/workflows/publish.yml, which installs R, renders the source pages, and
publishes the site to gh-pages. CNAME supplies the custom domain statcomp.org.

The CV publication section is generated from these sources:

  jyan.bib              Published and accepted works; also feeds the website.
  jyan-cv-extra.bib      CV-only reviews, manuscripts, and other contributions.
  software.qmd          Software titles, author metadata, and links.
  jyanCV.tex            Layout settings, the student roster, and other CV content.
  cv-trainees.json       Name aliases, additional trainees, and rare overrides.
  cv-publications.csl    Formatting for publication and software entries.

Do not edit cv-publications.tex or publications.qmd directly. Both are generated,
ignored by Git, and kept out of version control. The Quarto pre-render hook runs
make publications so a clean checkout can render or preview the website. The CV
build regenerates cv-publications.tex before compiling with the local vita class.

Commands
--------

  make publications           Regenerate the website's publications.qmd.
  make cv-publications        Regenerate cv-publications.tex without LaTeX.
  make                        Regenerate both lists and render the website.
  make cv                     Regenerate CV publications, compile jyanCV.pdf,
                              and copy it into bindocs/.
  make check-cv-publications   Fail if the local CV fragment is missing or stale.
  python3 -m unittest discover -s tests
                              Check generation and trainee-matching rules.
  quarto preview              Preview the website locally.

Dependencies are Quarto (including Pandoc), Python 3 with its standard library,
and R for the website publication scripts. The CV PDF additionally requires
latexmk, a LaTeX installation with titlesec, enumitem, microtype, and etoolbox, and
vita.cls with vitaStatistics.clo. Generation and its tests do not require
LaTeX or network access. The CI check pins Quarto
1.10.18 for reproducible generated text; use that version locally when comparing
generated output.

Commit source files only. Generated publication lists, rendered HTML, PDFs,
LaTeX auxiliary files, caches, and the local bindocs/ CV directory stay untracked.
CI generates the CV fragment from scratch and checks reproducibility on pushes
and pull requests. The publishing workflow generates the website publication
page through the Quarto pre-render hook. CI does not build/deploy the CV PDF.
Compiling directly with latexmk uses the existing fragment; use make cv to
regenerate it first.

Categories and publication status
---------------------------------

BibTeX entry types supply defaults: books go under Books and Edited Volumes;
chapters and conference papers under Book Chapters and Conference Proceedings;
journal articles under Refereed Journal Articles.

For exceptions, add a CV tag to the BibTeX keywords field, preserving any other
keywords. For example:

  keywords = {cv:magazine},

Supported tags are cv:books, cv:chapters, cv:articles, cv:magazine, cv:review,
and cv:under-review. Chance and Significance articles, editorials, and R News
contributions use cv:magazine, displayed as Magazine Articles and Other
Contributions. Under-review records use @unpublished and cv:under-review.
Reviews describe the review you authored, including the reviewed book.

Forthcoming works retain the existing year = {2026+} convention. They appear
first in their category, followed by descending year and author order. When a
manuscript is accepted, move the record from jyan-cv-extra.bib into jyan.bib,
keep its citation key, remove cv:under-review, and update its year, journal,
and other publication details. Remove the plus sign and Forthcoming note when
final publication details become available. Duplicate keys across either
bibliography cause generation to fail.

Software
--------

Group software.qmd entries under language headings such as "## R packages"
and, when needed, "## Python packages". Each package has one heading:

  ### {package}: Short Description {#package}

Immediately below the heading, store authors in a hidden HTML comment using
BibTeX name syntax and "and" between authors:

  <!-- cv-authors: Doe, Jane and Yan, Jun -->

The generator uses the heading description and the existing [package-cran]
link definition. A package without that reference needs an explicit link:

  <!-- cv-url: https://example.org/package -->

CV entries appear under Software Packages, alphabetized by package name. Each
begins with the linked name and a language/distribution label, followed by
authors and the description: for example, "spice (R; GitHub)." or a future
"example (Python; PyPI)." Language comes from the enclosing language heading;
distribution comes from the URL. CRAN archive links are identified separately.
When a package is on CRAN and developed on GitHub/GitLab, the existing CRAN
link is used as its primary distribution source.

The metadata is hidden on the website. Missing authors/links and duplicate
package headings cause generation to fail. Include every package in the
website's summary table and identify its source (CRAN, CRAN archive, or a
repository host). Active CRAN entries have release/download badges; archived
and development entries have status labels. All package sections appear in
the CV, and a test checks that the table and package sections cover the same
names in alphabetical order.

Commit and star badges in the package descriptions use verified development
repositories. grasps, ionet, KMsurv, spice, and subsampling have GitHub badges;
copula links to R-Forge. Do not substitute the automated cran/* GitHub mirrors
for maintainer repositories: their commits describe CRAN imports rather than
development activity. rrpack, sgee, som, and tpr currently have no verified
maintainer GitHub/GitLab repository recorded here, so those badges are omitted.

The old CV's six missing packages have been added: subsampling, fgof, rbugs,
spice (formerly fcstat), grasps, and ionet. fgof and rbugs link to CRAN archives.
spice uses its current name and GitHub repository; the website retains the old
fcstat anchor for existing links.
The two existing website packages absent from the old CV, jds.rmd and rrpack,
are included too. Their author lists were checked against CRAN:
https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/jds.rmd/index.html
https://cran.rstudio.com/web/packages/rrpack/index.html
rrpack includes Jun Yan as a contributor in the package listing.

Trainee annotations
-------------------

An asterisk denotes a trainee coauthor. The student list in jyanCV.tex supplies
the roster and completed PhD graduation years. The post_phd_buffer_years map in
cv-trainees.json applies a two-year buffer to three academic alumni, inclusive:

  Marcos Prates (2011): mark through 2013; omit from 2014 onward.
  Steven Chiou (2013):  mark through 2015; omit from 2016 onward.
  Jackson Lautier (2023): mark through 2025; omit from 2026 onward.

Other alumni retain their former-trainee annotations without a general cutoff.
Each coauthor is evaluated separately; the paper and author list remain included.

Expected PhD dates denote current students and do not impose a cutoff.
Undergraduate work is always marked as trainee work, regardless of publication
year. Additional trainees without a recorded PhD year retain their annotations.
Forthcoming entries use the numeric part of their year (2026+ is evaluated as
2026). Undated software listings retain their historical
trainee annotations. Explicit author overrides take precedence over the rule.

cv-trainees.json provides spelling variants and preserves previous marks for
trainees absent from that list. Tao Wang and Feng Wang are excluded at the
owner's request. Full given names are compared when available, so Tao Wang and
Tianying Wang do not match Tiandong Wang. Initial-only names still require
care when people share a surname and initial.

If an author matches multiple trainees, generation fails. Prefer spelling out
the author's given name in the source. Alternatively, add an author_overrides
entry keyed by citation key (software uses software-package), then by the
one-based author position:

  "example2026": {
    "1": {"author": "Wang, T.", "trainee": true}
  }

The author string must match the BibTeX-parsed family and given names exactly.
Changing author order/name invalidates the override rather than silently
marking someone else. Set trainee to false to suppress a misleading match.

Migration notes to review
-------------------------

All 162 main-bibliography records and 23 CV-only records are represented once.
The generated categories contain 2 books, 7 chapters/proceedings, 149 journal
articles, 10 magazine/editorial contributions, 4 reviews, 29 software packages,
and 13 manuscripts under review. All main-bibliography records use jyan.bib's
publication details, which resolve some existing differences from the old CV.

The duplicate graphical-model review was reconciled against main.tex in the
fcggm/manuscript project: zhang2026graphical retains the current six-author list
and title; the obsolete Xiao-first xiao2025graphical entry was removed. The
existing 2026 manuscript year is retained; the project's 2027 directory name
does not by itself establish a different manuscript year.

CV layout (2026-11-15)
----------------------

The CV uses ordinary unnumbered section/subsection commands and description or
numbered lists. The publication generator emits the same standard markup.
The trainee parser reads the Students section, up to the following section.
There are no custom category definitions or environment mappings to maintain.

The jyanCV.tex preamble uses titlesec's small/compact heading presets, enumitem
for shared lists and protection against breaks immediately after headings, and
microtype's default text fitting. The bottomtitlespace setting defines a
four-line bottom exclusion zone for headings; there are no manual page breaks.
List entries retain the default flexible gap, 4pt plus 2pt minus 1pt, while
paragraph spacing inside entries is zero. The validated PDF has 21 pages and no
section/subsection heading stranded at a page bottom.

The installed vita class supplies the standard title and full-width, two-column
address block; its Business Address/Home Address labels are suppressed locally.
Neither the installed vita.cls nor vitaStatistics.clo is modified. The supplied
old reference class is preserved as vita-old.cls; it must
not be named vita.cls here, because TeX would load it instead of the installed
class. Exact reproduction of the old layout is no longer the objective.

PDF hosting
-----------

bindocs/ contains only the binary CV, jyanCV.pdf. It is uploaded separately to
S3 with make push. The website pages and other assets are published through
GitHub Actions to gh-pages. The website links to the CV at
https://statds.org/doc/bindocs/jyanCV.pdf.
The AWS CLI uses the named profile statds and the existing bucket statds.org:

  Local CV folder:  bindocs/
  S3 CV folder:     s3://statds.org/doc/bindocs/
  CV object:        s3://statds.org/doc/bindocs/jyanCV.pdf
  Public CV:        https://statds.org/doc/bindocs/jyanCV.pdf

New computer: install the AWS CLI
---------------------------------

Install AWS CLI v2 using AWS's instructions:
https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/getting-started-install.html

Check the architecture with uname -m. Linux aarch64/arm64 uses the ARM installer;
Linux x86_64 uses the x86 installer. For Linux ARM64, a per-user installation is:

  curl -L https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-aarch64.zip -o /tmp/awscliv2.zip
  unzip -q /tmp/awscliv2.zip -d /tmp/awscli-installer
  mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/bin"
  /tmp/awscli-installer/aws/install --install-dir "$HOME/.local/aws-cli" --bin-dir "$HOME/.local/bin"
  export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
  aws --version

For Linux x86_64, substitute awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip in the download URL.
Use a fresh extraction directory if /tmp/awscli-installer already exists.
Persist the PATH line in your shell startup file if ~/.local/bin is not already
on PATH. For an existing installation at these paths, use the install command
with --update. On macOS or Windows, follow the corresponding AWS instructions.

New computer: restore the statds profile
---------------------------------------

The Makefile expects the profile name statds; configuring only default is not
enough. If the old computer used access keys, either transfer its existing
statds profile securely from ~/.aws/config and ~/.aws/credentials, or configure
the existing authorized identity interactively on the new computer:

  aws configure --profile statds

You may also securely copy the old ~/.aws/ directory into the new computer's
home directory when you intend to reuse the same AWS identity. Preserve any
profiles already configured on the new computer rather than overwriting them.
For this access-key profile, only config and credentials are needed; the CLI
recreates cli/cache/ itself. Do not transfer caches as a substitute for an SSO
login; run aws sso login again if using SSO. Copy the files, rather than moving
them, to keep the old computer's configuration intact.

Enter the access key ID and secret access key locally, the bucket's region
(from the old configuration or the S3 console), and json for output format.
The bucket's region is not recorded in this repository; do not infer it from
the bucket name. Keep credentials in ~/.aws/, outside this repository.
For transferred credential/config files, restrict access locally:

  chmod 700 ~/.aws
  chmod 600 ~/.aws/config ~/.aws/credentials

If the existing AWS identity uses IAM Identity Center / SSO instead of access
keys, set up that login method under the same profile name:

  aws configure sso --profile statds
  aws sso login --profile statds

Profile documentation:
https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-files.html
https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-sso.html

Verify the profile and bucket access without uploading:

  aws configure list-profiles
  aws sts get-caller-identity --profile statds
  aws s3 ls s3://statds.org/doc/bindocs/ --profile statds

The identity check confirms which AWS identity is active; the S3 listing checks
access to the CV folder. A profile-not-found error means statds needs setup;
an expired SSO token needs a fresh aws sso login. AccessDenied on S3 requires
the appropriate bucket/prefix permissions for the configured identity. Listing
and mirroring need ListBucket; downloads need GetObject; uploads need PutObject;
the deleting mirror also needs DeleteObject. Reuse the existing authorized
identity and bucket configuration rather than changing public-access settings.

Routine CV update
-----------------

From this repository, run:

  make cv
  make push

make cv builds jyanCV.pdf and copies it to bindocs/. Review that PDF before
running make push, which syncs the CV folder to the S3 prefix with --delete.
This prefix contains only the CV; the website's GitHub Actions deployment is
separate. Open the public CV URL after uploading to verify the result.

make pull is optional: it downloads the hosted CV into bindocs/ and can overwrite
that local copy. It is not needed before publishing a newly built CV.

AWS command references:
https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/s3/sync.html
https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/sts/get-caller-identity.html

Building the website does not upload PDFs. No upload is part of CV generation
or its CI check; uploads occur only through the explicit push targets/commands.
